592186529076 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529076 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529076 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186528506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529076 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eb700264-272d-54a5-aa96-05b215519e4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529091 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529091 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106044" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529091 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529091 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cf/cf3840cca56d90bc6b3981d15ead9c4ae45665693ef5c7794e65574204e632db.png)\n\nThe equation of the curve above is $y={x}^{2}-3x+k.$ Find the value of $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529091 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1100bf2f-961a-5344-8257-63842e33b322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529091 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that if we substitute $x=0$ into the given equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}-3x+k \\\\ y & =(0)^{2}-3(0)+k \\\\ y & =k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nmeaning that the curve intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,k).$ Based on the graph, we see that we must have $k=2$ since our parabola intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529097 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529097 #edb/ref 1069 "q-24639" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529097 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529097 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/28/28032a665a1266f06292394bc9a238b196c25ef9e8d275009e3d7587a1511c6e.png)\n\nFind the roots of the parabola shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529097 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1834012f-4a0b-5e03-8f32-bee5b9948e5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529097 #edb/ref 1074 "The roots of a parabola are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the curve intercepts the $x$-axis at the point $(-1,0).$\n\nTherefore, the root is $x=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529123 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529123 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106057" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529123 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529123 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following shows the graph of $y=2{x}^{2}+4x-3?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529123 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53ebdab8-abca-5063-be77-653bbbd15d1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529123 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's construct a table of values for the function $y=2{x}^{2}+4x-3.$ We fill the first row with the $x$-values $x=-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,$ and then use the formula $y=2{x}^{2}+4x-3$ to compute the corresponding $y$-values in the second row.\n\n| $\\,\\,x\\,\\,$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,\\,y\\,\\,$ | $3$ | $-3$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $3$ | $13$ |\n\nIf we plot those points on a graph, we can start to see the shape of a parabola:\n\n![](images/b4/b43e0f6b6f359b08dd346a30509d82482a3b82defac90728ddaa7c9769690846.png)\n\nWe fill out the graph by drawing the parabola through the plotted points:\n\n![](images/8d/8de7d3c268e35f85da8f1bf7882d2ebd285fa3f5c0c239c986eeb1564277e4e1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529126 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529126 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106054" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529126 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529126 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following shows the graph of $y=-{x}^{2}+10x-28?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529126 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "57d98e6f-6b53-5ed4-abd4-c02ceb5b88f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529126 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's construct a table of values for the function $y=-{x}^{2}+10x-28.$ We fill the first row with the $x$-values $x=3,4,5,6,7,8,$ and then use the formula $y=-{x}^{2}+10x-28$ to compute the corresponding $y$-values in the second row.\n\n| $\\,\\,x\\,\\,$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,\\,y\\,\\,$ | $-7$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-4$ | $-7$ | $-12$ |\n\nIf we plot those points on a graph, we can start to see the shape of a parabola:\n\n![](images/b7/b762d214dbd72555b069846ca8e7bcb5248c73c15db10f06018167ff7966d6d0.png)\n\nWe fill out the graph by drawing the parabola through the plotted points:\n\n![](images/24/24aae5c27fc48b7a340e36f6a54d9d6d636644c938e5328c1a8c68713179a947.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529127 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529127 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106029" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529127 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529127 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/68/687ea4a6fac8c4c6b7df7336f13b8ca39eb1b7049990c9875eeeaf556bd5353c.png)\n\nFor the parabola shown above, find the vertex and the equation of the axis of symmetry." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529127 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a174f7c-09d0-50e5-afac-66bf0cc4c315" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529127 #edb/ref 1074 "The vertex is the \"turning point\" of the parabola. In an upwards parabola, like the one here, the vertex is the maximum point. So, the vertex of this parabola is $(5,6).$\n\nThe axis of symmetry is the vertical line that passes through the vertex. Therefore, the axis of symmetry of this parabola is $x=5.$\n\n![](images/ed/ed00e2896127e6f3da7fc8e63c22e1944434060ac53e4e63d31f1cd7590a8627.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529151 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529151 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106039" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529151 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529151 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9e/9e207974b8c7b872864cce6e3ef80335ed0c9d20ec1b10960d36fb6a86971878.png)\n\nFind the roots of the parabola shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529151 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a08fd8f-f28b-522d-892c-2595551ed22c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529151 #edb/ref 1074 "The roots of a parabola are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the curve does not intercept the $x$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the parabola does not have any roots." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529164 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529164 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106037" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529164 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529164 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/dc/dc388ceccdd95990d69ebf1c2df36ab8ed0c83228d7684b13a7a791e9705851f.png)\n\nFind the roots of the parabola shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529164 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba657a54-9357-5409-90cb-ee552f02dde2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529164 #edb/ref 1074 "The roots of a parabola are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the curve intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-5,0)$ and $(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=-5$ and $x=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529178 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529178 #edb/ref 1069 "q-30782" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529178 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529178 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/05/05213dec3ea87b199cdd6c4bd73f5dedc3e19df69fed5ceef4a44dda60365b4b.png)\n\nFind the roots of the parabola shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529178 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d8dc19dd-4912-57fc-b7e0-84c553fa7c79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529178 #edb/ref 1074 "The roots of a parabola are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the curve intercepts the $x$ axis at the points $(3,0)$ and $(6,0).$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=3$ and $x=6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
